MAINTAINER DECISION. Found 2026-08-28 while building k79 step 6 for
containers; a test in t/containers_endpoints.t caught it.

Measured:

  API::Docker::Type::ContainerInspectResponse->new(Id => "x", State => "exited")
  -> dies, Error::TypeTiny::Assertion
     Value "exited" did not pass type constraint
     Maybe[InstanceOf["API::Docker::Type::ContainerState"]] (in $args->{"state"})

So a single field whose value disagrees with spec/v1.51.yaml makes the whole
response unusable, not just that field. This is a property of the model as a
whole (any object-, array- or scalar-typed attribute), not of containers.

It contradicts what the model's own POD says it does:

  API::Docker::Type, _isa_for:  "croaking while inflating a response we could
  otherwise use is not an improvement"
  API::Docker::Type, on `required`: "the same engines omit fields the
  specification calls required, and croaking on a response we could otherwise
  use is not an improvement"
  API::Docker::Role::Type, unknown_fields: "a field the caller set must never
  be dropped because we have not heard of it"

and it is the same class of risk k79's decision 2 rules out for version
gating: we are not the authority on what an engine answers. Podman announces
1.44 and Docker 1.55 on this machine; the model is generated from 1.51.

Where it already bites: API::Docker::API::Containers::_assert_container_running
is attach's pre-flight guard, documented and measured as fail-open ("a guard
that is unsure must not be the thing that breaks a working call" -- attaching
to a stopped container destroys its exit status on Podman). It now has to
eval the inspect and swallow an Error::TypeTiny to keep that contract. That
workaround is one method deep; the property is 201 classes wide.

The decision, which is not an agent's to make:

  a) leave it -- a response that disagrees with the swagger is a hard error
  b) keep the offending value in unknown_fields under its wire name, so the
     object inflates and nothing is lost (matches the passthrough invariant
     and k81)
  c) keep the raw value in the attribute and drop the isa constraint for
     object-typed fields

If (b) or (c): API::Docker::API::Containers::_assert_container_running loses
its eval, and t/containers_endpoints.t's "a State that is a string" case
starts proving the model rather than the workaround.

Lane: api-docker-type-writer (the DSL), with api-docker-engine-worker for
whether any engine really answers a shape the swagger does not describe.


MEASURED 2026-08-28 by the orchestrating agent, to answer this ticket's own
open question ("whether any engine really answers a shape the swagger does
not describe"). Read-only probes against the rootless Podman on this machine
(5.8.4); nothing was created, started or removed.

  Real Podman responses fed through their generated class:
    SystemVersion   inflates
    SystemInfo      inflates; 7 fields the swagger does not have --
                    BuildahVersion, CPURealtimePeriod, CPURealtimeRuntime,
                    Rootless, SwapFree, SwapTotal, Uptime
    ImageSummary    inflates (5 objects); 5 unknown -- Dangling, Digest,
                    History, Names, VirtualSize
    ImageInspect    inflates; 2 unknown -- ContainerConfig, VirtualSize
    Network (list and inspect)  inflates; nothing unknown
  No container or volume existed to probe, and creating one is a write.

  NOT ONE type conflict in any of them. Engines diverge from the swagger in
  CONTENT constantly -- 14 distinct unknown fields on one idle machine -- and
  the passthrough invariant already handles that without a croak.

  Docker's own swagger almost never changes a field's type: across v1.41 ->
  v1.51 exactly two fields changed shape, and both are the same shape renamed
  (ImageInspect.GraphDriver ref:GraphDriverData -> ref:DriverData;
  Task.Status inline object -> ref:TaskStatus). So version drift is not a
  source of type conflicts either.

  And the case that triggered this ticket is SYNTHETIC. t/containers_endpoints.t
  line 576's `State => 'exited'` sits in a list headed "Anything the check
  cannot read is not evidence that the container is stopped" -- it was written
  as a defensive shape for the guard, not captured from an engine.

WHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT SETTLE

  It does not argue for (a). It argues that (b) is cheap: the case is rare,
  and a rare event that makes a whole usable response unusable is exactly the
  kind you do not want, while a rare passthrough costs nothing. Rarity is an
  argument for the lenient option, not against it.

  Recommendation: (b). It is continuous with the passthrough invariant that
  already exists and is already tested; a typed attribute stays trustworthy
  (if set, it is the declared type) which (c) gives up; nothing is lost, since
  the value is kept under its wire name and TO_JSON writes it back; and
  _assert_container_running loses its eval.


INDEPENDENT SECOND OPINION 2026-08-28 (Codex, read-only cross-check),
which sharpens (b) rather than agreeing with it:

  Take (b), but ONLY for from_data. Keep new strict.

  The reasoning: the two entry points have different obligations. from_data
  inflates an engine RESPONSE -- the library is obliged to salvage what it
  can, and a hard error there makes every other field of a usable inspect
  inaccessible over one divergent Podman field. new builds a REQUEST out of
  caller-authored data, where a value that disagrees with the spec is a typo
  worth dying on, not an engine being itself.

    from_data({ Id => "x", State => "exited" })
      ->id                        "x"
      ->state                     undef
      ->unknown_fields->{State}   "exited"
    new(State => "exited")        still dies

  Against (c): dropping the isa on object-typed fields destroys the accessor's
  contract -- a caller could never know whether ->state is a ContainerState, a
  string or an array -- and it does not even work as written, because the
  serialiser calls $value->TO_JSON unconditionally, so inflation would succeed
  and serialisation would then die instead.

  What (b) costs, stated plainly: the typed accessor is unset although the
  field was present, so a caller who wants the raw value must know to look in
  unknown_fields; and "absent" becomes indistinguishable from "present but
  rejected" unless that is recorded separately.

  It also forces the precedence question in k85 defect 4: TO_JSON currently
  lets a known field overwrite a same-named entry in unknown_fields, so a
  preserved mismatched value would be silently dropped on the way back out.
  Decide that together with this, or give rejected values their own store.


DECIDED 2026-08-28 by the maintainer: OPTION (b), READ SIDE ONLY.

  from_data({ Id => "x", State => "exited" })
    ->id                       "x"
    ->state                    undef
    ->unknown_fields->{State}  "exited"    under the WIRE name
    ->TO_JSON                  carries State => "exited" unchanged

  new(State => "exited")       still dies

The two entry points keep different obligations: from_data inflates an engine
RESPONSE and salvages what it can, new builds a REQUEST out of caller-authored
data where a disagreeing value is a typo worth dying on.

Two conditions attached to the implementation:
  - a rejected value must stay distinguishable from one that was never sent;
    if that is not visible through unknown_fields alone, the POD says so.
  - the leniency hangs only off the response path.

Being implemented together with k85 defect 1, since both turn on what
from_data does with a value it cannot use. k85 defect 4 (the TO_JSON
precedence) folds in with it.

Implemented as option (b), read side only, in dc59680, together with k85 defect 1 -- the two were the same question. Measured after: from_data({Id=>'x',State=>'exited'}) gives ->id 'x', ->state undef, ->rejected_fields {State=>'state'}, and TO_JSON carrying State=>'exited' unchanged; new(State=>'exited') still dies. Both of the maintainer's conditions met: a rejected field is distinguishable from one never sent, through the new rejected_fields attribute, and the leniency hangs on the from_data entry point rather than on a flag -- pinned by a test that constructs under the flag and asserts new still croaks. One property to know: rejection is field-granular, so one bad element rejects its whole array and keeps it raw.
